  <h1>Sub-4 Hour Marathon: Three Pace Plans That Actually Work</h1>
  <div class="postmeta">August 23, 2026 · Training</div>

  <article>
    <p>Four hours is the marathon's most crowded finish line for a reason. It is
    fast enough to demand respect and slow enough to be within reach of almost
    any runner willing to train. The math is simple: <strong>9:09 per mile
    (5:41 per km) is your ceiling</strong>. The strategy is where people blow it.</p>

    <p>Here are three ways to get there. Two are the textbook and the pro
    playbook. The third is what I actually do, which I do not necessarily
    recommend, but I will not pretend I run these things by the book either.
    Every plan below opens live in the calculator, so you can tap it, tweak it,
    and make it yours.</p>

    <h2>Plan 1: The Textbook Even Split</h2>
    <div class="plan">
      <h3>Run 9:05s. All of them.</h3>
      <p class="stats">Every mile at 9:05 &middot; finish 3:58:00 &middot; 2:00 of cushion</p>
      <p>The most boring plan in running and the one with the best success rate.
      Lock in a pace a few seconds under your ceiling, run it from the gun, and
      trust it when it feels insultingly easy at mile 6. It is supposed to feel
      easy at mile 6. The runners who bank time in the first half give it back
      with interest in the second, and the ones who run metronome splits pass
      them all after mile 20.</p>
      <a class="plan-btn" href="/?d=marathon&amp;u=mi&amp;mode=even&amp;t=3:58:00">Open this plan in the calculator</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Plan 2: The Pro-Style Negative Split</h2>
    <div class="plan">
      <h3>Patient for 20, brave for 10K.</h3>
      <p class="stats">20 mi at 9:12, last 10K at 8:45 &middot; finish 3:58:25</p>
      <p>This is how the best races at every level are run: start a touch slower
      than goal pace, spend 20 miles collecting runners' souls being patient,
      then squeeze the last 10K. The hard part is not the fast finish. It is
      having the discipline to run 9:12 at mile 3 when your legs are begging for
      8:40. If you have done the long runs and the mileage, this plan turns the
      last 10K from a death march into the best part of your race.</p>
      <a class="plan-btn" href="/?d=marathon&amp;u=mi&amp;mode=custom&amp;b1_dist=20&amp;b1_pace=9:12&amp;b2_dist=6.22&amp;b2_pace=8:45">Open this plan in the calculator</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Plan 3: The Hold-On Method</h2>
    <div class="plan founder">
      <h3>My confession: reasonable half, then survive.</h3>
      <p class="stats">13 mi at 8:45, miles 14-20 at 9:05, last 10K at 9:30 &middot; finish 3:56:25</p>
      <p>Here is the truth about how I run marathons. I race a lot of 5Ks and
      half marathons. My 5K pace is around 7:30. What I mostly do not do is the
      20-mile training run, or all the runs that would really get me where I
      need to be. So my race plan is built around that honesty.</p>
      <p>I go out and run a reasonable half marathon, somewhere in the 8:30 to
      9:00 range, nothing heroic. After 13, the job changes: do not slip past
      9:00 if I can help it. As 20 gets close, the deal I make with myself is
      to stay near 9:30. And then I hold on and find out what is left in the
      tank. Some days that is a strong last 10K and a great finish. Some days
      the tank has other opinions.</p>
      <p>I am not recommending this. Sergeant Stride will warn you about the fast start the
    moment you open my plan, and he is right. But if your
      training looks like mine, short-race fit and long-run light, at least
      plan the fade on purpose instead of being surprised by it. That is the
      difference between holding on and falling apart.</p>
      <a class="plan-btn" href="/?d=marathon&amp;u=mi&amp;mode=custom&amp;b1_dist=13&amp;b1_pace=8:45&amp;b2_dist=7&amp;b2_pace=9:05&amp;b3_dist=6.22&amp;b3_pace=9:30&amp;profile=1.100">Open my plan in the calculator</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Which plan is yours?</h2>
    <p>It depends on who you are as a runner, and being honest about that is
    the whole game. If you have done the mileage and the long runs, Plan 1 or
    Plan 2 will serve you well. If you are a short-distance specialist trying
    to gut one out on 5K fitness, you are living in Plan 3 territory whether
    you admit it or not, so admit it and plan for it.</p>
    <p>We wrote up the runner types and the math behind all of this, including
    why standard race predictors overestimate runners who skip long runs, in
    <a href="/about">How We Built It</a>. Find your type, set the profile
    slider honestly, and let the calculator tell you the truth before mile 22
    does.</p>
